{"timestamp":"2026-09-09T16:14:45.428Z","ordinal":12508,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"task_complete","turn_id":"01a082bc-1971-70f0-aecc-959aea46ada6","last_agent_message":"Yes—I ran both:\n\n- The paid E2E test, which invokes `copilot -p` through the Go harness.\n- A separate wrapper-free `copilot -p` smoke test in an isolated repository, with no test harness and no retries.\n\nThe direct test passed on Copilot CLI 1.0.63:\n\n- Copilot launched the named `entire-direct-smoke` subagent.\n- The subagent created `docs/direct-smoke.md`.\n- Entire recorded one completed task with ID `REDACTED`.\n- A normal Git commit created checkpoint `01M23F3ZP35KVBTYGRX3RJ1AQG`.\n- The permanent checkpoint ref contains the subagent’s `task.json`.\n- Parent checkpoint metadata includes the modified file.\n- The shadow branch was cleaned up afterward.\n\nThe isolated smoke repository is:\n\n`/private/tmp/copilot-direct-smoke.XMTUsb`\n\nOne version-specific detail: Copilot 1.0.63 does not provide per-file telemetry in its subagent events, so the task record correctly marks its child transcript unavailable while the parent checkpoint records the changed file. Copilot 1.0.82’s newer event format supports exact child-file attribution.\n\nSo this is now validated as an actual end-user flow—not only an E2E simulation. The feature-branch changes themselves remain uncommitted and unpushed.","started_at":1788899760,"completed_at":1788970485,"duration_ms":32384303,"time_to_first_token_ms":68694}}
